{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Why didn't my CloudTrail logs capture IAM user activity?","context":"### Check whether you created a trail for logs that are older than 90 days\n\nCloudTrail event history retains events for 90 days. To view events that are older than 90 days, you must [create a CloudTrail trail](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-create-a-trail-using-the-console-first-time.html). If you didn't create a trail before the activity occurred, then you can't retrieve the event.\n\nTo determine whether you created a trail, choose **Trails** in the navigation pane of the [CloudTrail console](https://console.aws.amazon.com/cloudtrail) and check for a list of trails. If a trail exists, then check the associated Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) bucket for the logs that are older than 90 days.\n\nCloudTrail stores IAM events under the **us-east-1** prefix. To events from other services, find the prefix that matches the Region where you called the API operation. To update an existing trail to multi- or single-Region, see [Converting a single-Region trail to a multi-Region trail](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-create-and-update-a-trail-by-using-the-aws-cli-update-trail.html#cloudtrail-create-and-update-a-trail-by-using-the-aws-cli-examples-convert) or [Converting a multi-Region trail to a single-Region trail](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-create-and-update-a-trail-by-using-the-aws-cli-update-trail.html#cloudtrail-create-and-update-a-trail-by-using-the-aws-cli-examples-reduce).\n\nIf you created an [S3 Lifecycle configuration](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html), then check whether the [expiration action](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html) that you set deleted the log files","url":"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/cloudtrail-logs-iam-user-activity"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Working with CloudTrail event history","context":"# Working with CloudTrail event history\n\nCloudTrail is enabled by default for your AWS account and you automatically have access to the CloudTrail event history.\nThe event history provides a viewable, searchable, downloadable, and immutable record of the past 90 days of management events\nin an AWS Region. These events capture activity made through the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface, and AWS SDKs\nand APIs. The event history records events in the AWS Region where the event happened. There are no CloudTrail charges for viewing the event history.\n\nYou can look up events related to the creation, modification, or deletion of resources\n(such as IAM users or Amazon EC2 instances) in your AWS account on a by-Region basis on the CloudTrail console\nby viewing the **Event history** page. You can also look up these events by running the `aws cloudtrail\nlookup-events` command or by using the `LookupEvents` API.\n\nYou can use the **Event history** page on the CloudTrail console to view,\nsearch, download, archive, analyze, and respond to account activity across your AWS\ninfrastructure. You can customize the\nview of the **Event history** page on the console by selecting\nhow many events to display on each page and which columns to display or hide. You can also\ncompare the details of events in event history side-by-side. You can programmatically look up events by using the AWS SDKs or\nAWS Command Line Interface.\n\n**Note:**\n\nOver time, AWS services might add additional events. CloudTrail records these events in\nevent history, but a full 90-day record of activity that\nincludes added events won't be available until 90 days after it adds the events.\n\nThe event history is separate from any trails or event data\nstores that you create for your account. Changes you make to your event data stores or\ntrails do not affect the event history.\n\nThe sections which follow describe how to look up recent management events by using the CloudTrail console and the AWS CLI, and\ndescribe how to download a file of events. For information about using the\n`LookupEvents` API to retrieve information from CloudTrail events, see LookupEvents\nin the *AWS CloudTrail API Reference*.\n\n**Topics**\n\n* Limitations of Event history\n* Viewing recent management events with the console\n* Viewing recent management events with the AWS CLI","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/view-cloudtrail-events.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Multiple Trails – AWS CloudTrail FAQs – AWS","context":"#### Does the CloudTrail Event History show all account activity within my account?\n\nAWS CloudTrail will only show the results of the CloudTrail Event history for the current Region you are viewing for the last 90 days, and supports a range of AWS services . These events are limited to management events that create, modify, and delete API calls and account activity. For a complete record of account activity, including all management events, data events, and read-only activity, you must configure a CloudTrail trail","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/cloudtrail/faqs/"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Uncover blind spots in AWS data plane operations with CloudTrail Event Coverage - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail's new feature, Event Coverage, allows users to view their data plane operations coverage at the account and organization levels in the AWS CloudTrail console. This feature helps identify gaps in logging posture by showing which AWS services and resource types have data event logging enabled and which do not. Data events enable tracking of data plane operations in AWS services, such as Amazon S3 object-level operations, to detect unauthorized access or exfiltration attempts. Event Coverage provides a centralized view of coverage across all supported data event sources and enables subscribing to data events directly from the dashboard. This simplifies closing coverage gaps, especially for organizations managing multiple accounts. Event Coverage is available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS CloudTrail is supported. For more information on logging Data Events, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/aws-cloudtrail-event-coverage/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Analyze your CloudTrail events using natural language in Amazon Q Console - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail, a service used for recording API activity across AWS accounts for security auditing, compliance, and operational troubleshooting, now integrates with Amazon Q Console. This integration enables users to investigate AWS account activity using natural language. Users can ask Amazon Q Console about CloudTrail configuration, query logged events for security investigations, and troubleshoot operational issues without writing queries or manually parsing log files. With this integration, users can check if CloudTrail trails are properly configured, identify gaps in logging coverage, and confirm which data event sources are being tracked. For security concerns, users can ask about who accessed specific IAM roles, changes made to VPC configurations, and unauthorized access attempts. For operational troubleshooting, users can ask about who created or deleted resources, identify API calls generating errors, trace activity from specific IP addresses, or determine why bills spiked. Amazon Q Console can query CloudTrail trails, associated CloudWatch log groups, and event data stores on behalf of users, providing answers grounded in actual account activity. This integration is available in all AWS commercial regions where Amazon Q Console is supported. To get started, users can open Amazon Q in the AWS Management Console and ask questions about their CloudTrail configuration or account activity. For more information, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/cloudtrail-amazon-q-console/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS CloudTrail adds data event aggregation to simplify security monitoring - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail, a service that records API calls for AWS account activity, introduces a new feature called CloudTrail aggregated events. This feature simplifies monitoring and analysis of high-volume CloudTrail data events by consolidating them into 5-minute summaries. Security, compliance, and operations teams can efficiently identify trends like access frequency, error rates, and frequently used actions without processing individual events. Aggregations are available for API activity, resource access, and user activity summaries. Users can enable aggregation through the AWS console or CLI and choose from pre-built templates. The aggregation service is charged based on the number of CloudTrail data events analyzed. It is available in all commercial AWS Regions. With this feature, teams can answer questions like \"How has this user's activity changed over the past week?\" or \"What are the top actions being performed on this critical resource?\" without having to scan through massive amounts of CloudTrail data events","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/cloudtrail-data-event-aggregation-security-monitoring/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-investigation","skill_description":"Investigates what is happening in an AWS account from evidence already recorded there. Not for capturing new data from running code (snapshots of function arguments on a live service, Dynamic Instrumentation), questions answered from the CloudWatch Omni context graph or Application Signals service map, or building or changing infrastructure. Covers reading deployment records instead of the newest stored version (completed AppConfig deployments, the version an alias points to, a stack's current template), following a request through every service's own logs (exact per-layer fields, unique failed requests in an alarm's breach window, failure boundary per event), and answering questions from the account's data (exact counts and breakdowns, coverage of the requested range, exact filters and joins, the rule behind failed or unmatched records, verbatim values). Use to triage an alarm, error spike or failed request, trace a request across services, or count, break down, extract or join records.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, appconfig, lambda, cloudformation, dynamodb, s3, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, investigate, query, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability, operations","skill_name":"aws-investigation"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS docs on CloudTrail Event history limitations vs trails"}

{"id": "eaca136a-3b86-416e-b5be-5012331e071c", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm reviewing the trail list: JMERWIN_Org_Trail is the organization trail with multi-region coverage and custom event selectors, while IsengardTrail-DO-NOT-DELETE and nebula-data are local trails scoped to this account.

So the org trail delivers a baseline multi-region management-event log (plus whatever data events the custom selectors cover) to a bucket in the management account outside this account's control, while the three local trails add overlapping or supplemental coverage — nebula-data-events-trail for single-region data events, and IsengardTrail/nebula-single-region-trail largely duplicating management-event logging for a subset of regions. For the audit question, I note that Event History alone won't suffice since it's capped at 90 days, covers management events only, and is per-region rather than aggregated — so a trail or event data store is required for longer retention, data events, and insights.

"}]}
